      \"random-removal baseline\",\n502\t        \"permuted/shuffled-outcome control\",\n503\t        \"influence-function and AMIP-style dropping-data robustness metrics\",\n504\t        \"vote-rigging and adversarial manipulation of crowdsourced leaderboards\",\n505\t        \"bootstrap/confidence-interval based ranking uncertainty\"\n506\t      ],\n507\t      \"core_methods\": [\n508\t        \"Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood fitting on pairwise outcome counts\",\n509\t        \"first-order influence / approximate maximum influence perturbation for data-dropping robustness\",\n510\t        \"greedy removal search with exact-refit verification, prefix bisection and swap polish\",\n511\t        \"cell-aggregated (pair-count) weighted refits with relaxed removal search\",\n512\t        \"stationarity-LP over target ratings for minimal removal sets\"\n513\t      ],\n514\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n515\t        \"generic optimization or benchmarking surveys\",\n516\t        \"scientific method or paper-writing phrasing\",\n517\t        \"chronology of cycles, attempts or search history\",\n518\t        \"internal score names such as combined_score or rubric\",\n519\t        \"titles, authors or results of the withheld published paper on this research question, or pages citing it\",\n520\t        \"unrelated LLM architecture or training queries\"\n521\t      ],\n522\t      \"queries\": [\n523\t        {\n524\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n525\t          \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model paired comparisons maximum likelihood estimation ranking\",\n526\t          \"rationale\": \"Defines the scoring model refit exactly in every flip-budget verification; needs the original model paper and standard MLE/fitting references.\"\n527\t        },\n528\t        {\n529\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n530\t          \"query\": \"approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness\",\n531\t          \"rationale\": \"Closest methodological antecedent: first-order influence to find a small data subset whose removal changes a conclusion, which the greedy search re-linearises and verifies exactly.\"\n532\t        },\n533\t        {\n534\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n535\t          \"query\": \"understanding black-box predictions influence functions\",\n536\t          \"rationale\": \"Source of the influence-function machinery underlying the greedy removal ranking; reviewers will expect it as the first-order comparator.\"\n537\t        },\n538\t        {\n539\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n540\t          \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena open platform evaluating LLMs human preference\",\n541\t          \"rationale\": \"Describes the crowd-vote data (55k/140k battles) and the Bradley-Terry ranking pipeline being stress-tested.\"\n542\t        },\n543\t        {\n544\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n545\t          \"query\": \"judging LLM-as-a-judge MT-Bench human expert pairwise judgments\",\n546\t          \"rationale\": \"Provides the expert-judgment and GPT-4 judge comparison data, the small contrasting evaluation design with only a few models.\"\n547\t        },\n548\t        {\n549\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n550\t          \"query\": \"improving model ranking Chatbot Arena vote rigging\",\n551\t          \"rationale\": \"Adversarial manipulation of crowd votes is the deliberate-perturbation counterpart to the minimal-removal flip budgets reported.\"\n552\t        },\n553\t        {\n554\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n555\t          \"query\": \"leaderboard illusion Chatbot Arena evaluation practices sampling private testing\",\n556\t          \"rationale\": \"Documents data-access and sampling asymmetries that affect the same leaderboard, relevant for interpreting small flip budgets as fragility.\"\n557\t        },\n558\t        {\n559\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n560\t          \"query\": \"adversarial manipulation robustness rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n561\t          \"rationale\": \"Comparator literature on how few corrupted comparisons can change rankings from pairwise data, framing the removal-search results beyond LLM leaderboards.\"\n562\t        },\n563\t        {\n564\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n565\t          \"query\": \"Elo rating system relation to Bradley-Terry sports game outcomes\",\n566\t          \"rationale\": \"Connects the NBA Elo game-record data (sports evaluation design) to Bradley-Terry-type fitting, needed to justify cross-design comparison.\"\n567\t        },\n568\t        {\n569\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n570\t          \"query\": \"statistical inference uncertainty ranking from pairwise comparisons confidence intervals bootstrap\",\n571\t          \"rationale\": \"Flip budgets are interpreted against sampling noise (gap-to-noise); needs the ranking-uncertainty literature that leaderboard confidence intervals rely on.\"\n572\t        }\n573\t      ],\n574\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n575\t        \"large crowd-vote tables flip with a tiny removal fraction while the small expert-judgment table resisted flipping within the removal cap in the search phase\",\n576\t        \"influence-guided removal is far tighter than random removal, and permuted-outcome controls need larger budgets than real data\",\n577\t        \"three search variants tied on the search-stage score, so the exact-refit-verified greedy is already near the achievable budget on the large crowd table\",\n578\t        \"flip budget depends on the score gap relative to noise and on the evaluation design\"\n579\t      ],\n580\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n581\t    },\n582\t    {\n583\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n584\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n585\t        \"Chatbot Arena human preference battles (55k / 140k)\",\n586\t        \"MT-Bench human expert judgments\"\n587\t      ],\n588\t      \"central_topic\": \"Citation gaps in a paper on minimal removal sets (verified by exact Bradley-Terry refit) that flip adjacent-pair rankings of pairwise-comparison leaderboards: the Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench data and platforms, influence-function and case-deletion diagnostics behind the first-order removal ranking, and manipulation or corruption of crowd-vote and pairwise-comparison rankings.\",\n589\t      \"comparators\": [\n590\t        \"influence-only row removal (B2 comparator)\",\n591\t        \"adversarial manipulation and vote rigging of crowd-vote leaderboards\",\n592\t        \"robustness of pairwise-comparison rankings to corrupted comparisons\"\n593\t      ],\n594\t      \"core_methods\": [\n595\t        \"first-order (one-step Newton) influence of deleting a row on the Bradley-Terry score gap, with leave-one-out leverage correction\",\n596\t        \"case-deletion diagnostics (Cook's distance style) for logistic-type likelihoods\"\n597\t      ],\n598\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n599\t        \"generic optimization or benchmarking surveys\",\n600\t        \"scientific method or paper-writing phrasing\",\n601\t        \"chronology of cycles, attempts or search history\",\n602\t        \"internal score names such as combined_score or rubric\",\n603\t        \"titles, authors or results of the withheld published paper on this research question, or pages citing it\",\n604\t        \"unrelated LLM architecture or training queries\",\n605\t        \"further Bradley-Terry extensions, existence/consistency or uncertainty theory already covered by the bibliography\"\n606\t      ],\n607\t      \"queries\": [\n608\t        {\n609\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n610\t          \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference\",\n611\t          \"rationale\": \"Target arena-leaderboard-context (Introduction, Data): the opening claim that Bradley-Terry leaderboards are used to choose and announce models, and the Arena 55k/140k data, have no platform citation.\"\n612\t        },\n613\t        {\n614\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n615\t          \"query\": \"Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena human pairwise judgments\",\n616\t          \"rationale\": \"Target arena-leaderboard-context (Data): the MT-Bench human expert judgments and the Arena human-preference data release are used without citing the dataset\n617\t\n618\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n619\t```\n620\t\n621\t## manuscript source excerpt\n622\t\n623\t```tex\n624\t\\documentclass{article}\n625\t\n626\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n627\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n628\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n629\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n630\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n631\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n632\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n633\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n634\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n635\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n636\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n637\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n638\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n639\t}\n640\t\n641\t \n642\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n643\t\n644\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n645\t\\usepackage{url}\n646\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n647\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n648\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n649\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n650\t\n651\t\\newcommand{\\kcap}{k_{\\mathrm{cap}}}\n652\t\\newcommand{\\hi}{h}\n653\t\\newcommand{\\lo}{\\ell}\n654\t\n655\t\\title{How Few Comparisons Flip a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard Pair? Refit-Verified Removal Budgets on Crowd-Vote and Sports Data}\n656\t\\author{}\n657\t\n658\t\\begin{document}\n659\t\\maketitle\n660\t\n661\t\\begin{abstract}\n662\tPublic leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair after an exact ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Verified budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd table (at most 0.046\\% of comparisons) and 62 and 8 rows for the sports table (0.48\\% and 0.062\\%). Mid-table pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and random deletion flips the top pairs in 0 of 20 draws. However, the evaluator's recorded influence-only reference (whose construction we could not verify) matched the studied configuration's budget in all six held-out cases, so no measured reduction relative to that reference is observed; the crowd-versus-sports contrast is confounded with table size, participants and era. A six-model expert-judgment table did not flip within the cap in development runs, for an unknown reason. All findings are descriptive: four cases, two designs, one rating model.\n663\t\\end{abstract}\n664\t\n665\t\\section{Introduction}\n666\tLeaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n667\t\n668\tWe make this question operational. Given a comparison table and one adjacent pair of rated participants, the \\emph{removal budget} is the smallest number of rows whose deletion makes an exact refit of the same BT objective rank the lower participant above the upper one. Deletion is a deliberately weak perturbation (no inserted or relabelled votes), closely related to the ``dropping a little data'' robustness question of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. Because every proposed deletion set is checked by an exact refit, any reported budget is a certificate that a reversal is attainable with that many rows, and therefore an upper bound on the true minimum.\n669\t\n670\t\\paragraph{Research question.} Under an exact BT refit (ridge $0.01$, ties as half wins), what is the smallest removal set within a cap of $2\\%$ of all rows that reverses an adjacent top-of-table pair or an adjacent mid-table pair, and does this budget differ between a crowd-vote table and a sports-results table when both are measured by one verified protocol?\n671\t\n672\t\\paragraph{What we do.} We measure verified removal budgets on two held-out tables of real data: a deduplicated Chatbot Arena crowd-vote table and an NBA game-results table. We compare the studied search configuration, which orders rows by first-order influence on the rating gap and then refines the set with re-linearised greedy batches and a local polish, against three evaluator-owned comparators (random removal, first-order influence only, and opponent-targeted removal) and three validity controls (empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy). A separate pair of development tables, including a six-model expert-judgment table, is used to compare search configurations and is reported separately.\n673\t\n674\t\\paragraph{What we find.} (i) Budgets are small in all four held-out cases (4 to 62 of 135,630 crowd rows; 8 to 62 of 12,900 NBA rows), and mid pairs are cheaper than top pairs in both tables. (ii) As a share of comparisons the sports budgets are about 10 and 21 times larger, but the two top-pair budgets are the same number of rows (62), so this contrast largely reflects table size and is confounded with participants and era. (iii) The evaluator's recorded influence-only reference matched the studied configuration's budget in every held-out case; we could not verify how that reference is constructed, and the studied configuration's recorded budgets equal that reference's in all six held-out cases, so no measured reduction relative to it is observed. (iv) On the six-model expert-judgment development table, no flip was found within the cap, and our evidence cannot say whether that is a property of the data or of the search.\n675\t\n676\t\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified, evaluator-verified removal-budget protocol with comparators and controls, whose budgets are certificates rather than estimates. (2) Measured budgets for four held-out cases on two evaluation designs, with the crowd-versus-sports contrast and its confounds spelled out. (3) A negative result: the evaluator's recorded influence-only reference, whose construction is unverified, matches the studied configuration's budget in every held-out case. (4) An explicit scope statement: the claims concern the declared cases only, not confidence-interval-based rank changes, adversarial insertion or vote rigging, other rating models, or causal effects of evaluation design.\n677\t\n678\t\\section{Related Work}\n679\t\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.} The BT model has been extended to ties and within-pair order effects \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1977OnET}, to multivariate and two-dimensional comparisons \\citep{Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Causeur2005A2E}, to continuous outcomes and generalised score estimation \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM}, to covariates and sparse grouped designs \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA,Yan2011GROUPEDSP}, and to Bayesian formulations \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB,Hussain2013BayesianIO,Rizvi2026BayesianBF}. Conditions for existence of the maximum likelihood estimate and its consistency are studied by \\citet{Butler2004TheEO} and \\citet{Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, and ridge-penalised fitting is analysed by \\citet{Varin2024TractableRR}. Computation and links to priority derivation and PageRank are treated by \\citet{Vojnović2019ConvergenceRO,Sawadogo2019CombiningMA,Genest1999DerivingPF,Selby2024PageRankAT}. We use none of these extensions: our evaluator fixes a plain ridge-penalised BT objective in which a tie counts as half a win, a convention that differs from explicit tie models such as that of \\citet{Davidson1970OnET}. The ridge term keeps every refit well defined after rows are removed.\n680\t\n681\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty and rank inference.} Sampling-based uncertainty for BT scores and ranks is the subject of \\citet{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, \\citet{Makur2024MinimaxHT} (hypothesis testing), \\citet{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP} (partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons) and \\citet{Li2021ThePO} (estimation error under general comparison graphs). These works ask how sampling variability limits what a ranking can resolve. We instead fix the observed table and ask how few of its rows determine the order of one adjacent pair. This is a statement about a single realised data set, not about a population, and we report no confidence-interval-based rank events.\n682\t\n683\t\\paragraph{Data-dropping robustness.} \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} propose an automatic finite-sample metric for when removing a small fraction of data changes a statistical conclusion, for statistical conclusions. Our setting is a specific instance in spirit: the conclusion is the order of one adjacent pair under a BT fit, and the dropped fraction is capped at $2\\%$. We accept a removal set only after an exact refit reverses the pair. \\citet{Defresne2025PreferenceEF} use maximum-likelihood preference models for active elicitation, a different goal from auditing a fixed table.\n684\t\n685\t\\paragraph{Manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards.} \\citet{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858} study vote-rigging strategies against Chatbot Arena and their effect on model rankings. Our setting is a deletion-only perturbation checked by exact refit, with no adversary model, and we do not compare numbers with that work. Influence-based analysis of single training points is the tool behind our row ordering \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730}. Work on other evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on MT-Bench-style model-judge evaluation, is relevant background that is not in our verified reference set, and we make no claim of novelty relative to it.\n686\t\n687\t\\section{Problem Setup and Method}\n688\t\\subsection{Bradley--Terry leaderboards and the removal budget}\n689\tA comparison table has $N$ rows $(a_i,b_i,y_i)$ over $M$ participants, where $y_i\\in\\{\\text{$a$ wins},\\text{$b$ wins},\\text{tie}\\}$ is encoded as a score $s_i\\in\\{1,0,\\tfrac12\\}$. For ratings $\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^M$ and $d_i=\\theta_{a_i}-\\theta_{b_i}$ the fit minimises\n690\t\\begin{equation}\n691\tL(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Big[\\log\\!\\big(1+e^{d_i}\\big)-s_i d_i\\Big]+\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\lVert\\theta\\rVert^2,\\qquad \\lambda=0.01,\n692\t\\label{eq:bt}\n693\t\\end{equation}\n694\tand participants are ranked by $\\theta$. Let $\\hi$ be ranked directly above $\\lo$. For a removal set $R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ let $\\theta^{(-R)}$ be the exact minimiser of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} on the remaining rows. The pair \\emph{flips} if $\\theta^{(-R)}_{\\lo}>\\theta^{(-R)}_{\\hi}$. With cap $\\kcap=\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$, the \\emph{removal budget} is\n695\t\\begin{equation}\n696\tk^\\star=\\min\\{|R|:\\ |R|\\le\\kcap,\\ \\text{pair flips under }R\\},\n697\t\\end{equation}\n698\tand a search procedure is scored by $1-k/\\kcap$ if it returns a flipping set of size $k\\le\\kcap$ and $0$ otherwise. Because the score is linear in $k$, a score near $1$ means a budget far below the cap. We report budgets in two units: rows, and percent of all $N$ comparisons (derived as $k/N$). The evaluator's ``budget fraction'' metric is $k/\\kcap$, a fraction of the allowance and not of the table.\n699\t\n700\tTwo kinds of target are used. The \\emph{top} pair is ranks 1 and 2 of the full-table fit; the \\emph{mid} pair is ranks $\\lfloor M/2\\rfloor$ and $\\lfloor M/2\\rfloor+1$. A flip here is a reversal of the \\emph{point-estimate} order; no confidence-interval rank event is considered.\n701\t\n702\t\\subsection{Why influence ordering should help, and what we test}\n703\tDeleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\theta_{\\hi}-\\theta_{\\lo}$ as, in the manner of influence-function analyses of the effect of removing a training point \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730},\n704\t\\begin{equation}\n705\t\\Delta_i\\;\\approx\\;\\frac{(p_i-s_i)\\,(v_{a_i}-v_{b_i})}{1-w_i\\,q_i},\\qquad v=H^{-1}(e_{\\hi}-e_{\\lo}),\n706\t\\label{eq:infl}\n707\t\\end{equation}\n708\twhere $p_i=\\sigma(d_i)$, $w_i=p_i(1-p_i)$, $H$ is the Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} (including the ridge), and $q_i=x_i^\\top H^{-1}x_i$ with $x_i=e_{a_i}-e_{b_i}$ gives the leave-one-out leverage correction. Rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ close the gap fastest to first order. Our use of this approximation differs from a pure influence analysis in that it only orders candidate rows, and every set is then verified by an exact refit. Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} is a linearisation, so we ask whether re-linearising and polishing an influence order reduces the budget relative to the evaluator's influence-only reference.\n709\t\n710\t\\subsection{Studied configuration}\n711\tThe studied configuration (source in the staged evidence for the selected candidate) fits BT by damped Newton iterations with backtracking and an analytic gradient and dense Hessian, and then proceeds in three steps. (a) It ranks rows by Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} at the full-table fit and finds, by bisection over prefixes of that order with an exact refit at each probe, the smallest prefix of at most $\\kcap$ rows that flips the pair. (b) It runs a re-linearised greedy search: after each batch of removals it refits, recomputes Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} on the remaining rows, and removes a batch of $\\lceil 0.6\\,n_{\\mathrm{lin}}\\rceil$ rows, where $n_{\\mathrm{lin}}$ is the number of rows the linearised gains need to close the current gap; once the pair flips it bisects over prefixes of the insertion order. (c) It polishes the best set by drop-and-readd local search: it drops the least useful quarter of the incumbent set (halving that fraction after a failure, for at most 14 rounds), refills greedily under a strictly smaller size limit, and keeps any strictly smaller verified set. A set is accepted only if its own exact refit separates $\\theta_{\\lo}$ above $\\theta_{\\hi}$ by a margin of $10^{-5}$; the evaluator records its own verified budget for the returned set. Search stops at 60\\% of the allotted deadline. Bisection over prefixes assumes that longer prefixes flip once shorter ones do; this is not guaranteed, so the returned $k$ is a verified upper bound on the minimum and minimality is not claimed.\n712\t\n713\t\\subsection{Comparators and validity controls}\\label{sec:method_comp}\n714\tThe evaluator reports three comparators for each case, scored with the same rule as the studied configuration: \\textbf{B1}, random removal (20 random orders per case; we report how many flip within the cap); \\textbf{B2}, an influence-only removal, which the evaluator exposes as its ``influence reference''; and \\textbf{B3}, an opponent-targeted removal. The evaluator source is not part of the staged evidence, so we do not independently verify how B1--B3 are constructed. We rely on their recorded outcomes (flip counts and budgets) and on the names under which they are recorded. B2's construction is therefore unverified; in particular we do not assert that it equals step (a) above, and we draw no conclusion that depends on that reading. B3 is described by the evaluator only as an opponent-targeted removal.\n715\t\n716\tThree controls are recorded alongside the cases: an \\emph{empty removal set}, which should not flip any case; a \\emph{label-permutation} control on the top pair of each table, for which the evaluator records the fraction of moved labels against its expectation, the rating-spread ratio relative to the real fit, and a non-vacuity indicator; and a \\emph{cross-table copy} control, for which it records the number of flips over its trials. We report only these recorded outcomes. The evidence does not state the thresholds or construction details behind the non-vacuity indicator or the copy control, and we make no claim about them.\n717\t\n718\t\\subsection{Why these definitions}\n719\tThe perturbation scale and the cross-dataset comparison are choices, so we state the reasons for them. \\emph{Deletion only:} every remaining row is a genuine observation, so a flip cannot be blamed on fabricated data, and an exact refit turns each deletion set into a checkable certificate. Insertion, relabelling and vote rigging would require an adversary model that we do not specify. \\emph{Adjacent pairs:} if a removal set puts $\\lo$ above $\\hi$ and the original ranking placed other participants between them, then at least one adjacent pair in that chain must also have reversed. The smallest budget over adjacent pairs therefore lower-bounds the budget of any non-adjacent reversal, and adjacent pairs are the cheapest rank events. \\emph{Top and mid:} the top pair is the position that leaderboards publicise, and the mid pair probes a typical interior position. \\emph{A 2\\% cap:} it fixes a common tolerance for how much of a table noise or an actor could plausibly alter and makes scores comparable across tables of different size. It is a convention, not an estimate of any real process, and we did not vary it; all held-out budgets sit well below it except the sports top pair, which uses 24\\% of its allowance. \n720\t\n721\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n722\t\\subsection{Data}\\label{sec:data}\n723\tTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. The search procedure receives rows as integer participant indices with a coded outcome; names are not visible to it in the inspected candidate source. The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release (data from the platform of \\citealp{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}) after content-hash deduplication against the development tables: from 135,634 raw rows, 2 were removed for content overlap with development data (a further 652 rows are listed in the evaluator metadata as prompt-only overlaps and were kept) and 2 rows of participants with fewer than 100 rows were dropped, leaving 135,630 rows over 52 models. The sports table holds NBA game results from 2006 to 2015 with 12,900 comparisons over 30 teams. The development tables are the Chatbot Arena 55k release (57,477 rows, 64 models) and the MT-Bench human-judgment table (3,355 rows, 6 models), the latter standing in for expert annotation. Dataset commits and licences come from the public task brief and are listed in the Supplement. Model-judge data were not used.\n724\t\n725\t\\begin{table}[t]\n726\t\\centering\n727\t\\caption{Comparison tables. Caps are $\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$ (derived). Held-out tables were never used to choose a search configuration; development tables were.}\n728\t\\label{tab:data}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25e0a1e8-b2dc-41ba-9c50-c6306ddb5c02","uuid":"71e874d9-c26f-49e0-bc52-96af67d9c373","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:39:17.447Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.m