{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P1Znjz4sD2kgM2A91DuGQF","type":"tool_result","content":"98\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n99\t\\subsection{Data}\\label{sec:data}\n100\tTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. The search procedure receives rows as integer participant indices with a coded outcome; names are not visible to it in the inspected candidate source. The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release (data from the platform of \\citealp{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}) after content-hash deduplication against the development tables: from 135,634 raw rows, 2 were removed for content overlap with development data (a further 652 rows are listed in the evaluator metadata as prompt-only overlaps and were kept) and 2 rows of participants with fewer than 100 rows were dropped, leaving 135,630 rows over 52 models. The sports table holds NBA game results from 2006 to 2015 with 12,900 comparisons over 30 teams. The development tables are the Chatbot Arena 55k release (57,477 rows, 64 models) and the MT-Bench human-judgment table (3,355 rows, 6 models), the latter standing in for expert annotation. Dataset commits and licences come from the public task brief and are listed in the Supplement. Model-judge data were not used.\n101\t\n102\t\\begin{table}[t]\n103\t\\centering\n104\t\\caption{Comparison tables. Caps are $\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$ (derived). Held-out tables were never used to choose a search configuration; development tables were.}\n105\t\\label{tab:data}\n106\t\\small\n107\t\\begin{tabular}{llllrrr}\n108\t\\toprule\n109\tUse & Design & Table & Rows $N$ & Participants & Cap $\\kcap$\\\\\n110\t\\midrule\n111\tHeld-out & crowd votes & Arena 140k (deduplicated) & 135,630 & 52 models & 2,713\\\\\n112\tHeld-out & sports & NBA 2006--2015 & 12,900 & 30 teams & 258\\\\\n113\tDevelopment & crowd votes & Arena 55k & 57,477 & 64 models & 1,150\\\\\n114\tDevelopment & expert judgments & MT-Bench human & 3,355 & 6 models & 68\\\\\n115\t\\bottomrule\n116\t\\end{tabular}\n117\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]] held-out rows, models, caps\n118\t% @evidence[[derived/task_meta/final_meta.json]] dedup counts\n119\t% @evidence[[derived/task_meta/search_meta.json]] development table sizes\n120\t\\end{table}\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25e0a1e8-b2dc-41ba-9c50-c6306ddb5c02","uuid":"d265a57d-f417-49c2-8964-f8854b44164d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:40:35.968Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n\\subsection{Data}\\label{sec:data}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. The search procedure receives rows as integer participant indices with a coded outcome; names are not visible to it in the inspected candidate source. The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release (data from the platform of \\citealp{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}) after content-hash deduplication against the development tables: from 135,634 raw rows, 2 were removed for content overlap with development data (a further 652 rows are listed in the evaluator metadata as prompt-only overlaps and were kept) and 2 rows of participants with fewer than 100 rows were dropped, leaving 135,630 rows over 52 models. The sports table holds NBA game results from 2006 to 2015 with 12,900 comparisons over 30 teams. The development tables are the Chatbot Arena 55k release (57,477 rows, 64 models) and the MT-Bench human-judgment table (3,355 rows, 6 models), the latter standing in for expert annotation. Dataset commits and licences come from the public task brief and are listed in the Supplement. Model-judge data were not used.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Comparison tables. Caps are $\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$ (derived). Held-out tables were never used to choose a search configuration; development tables were.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\s